// The receiver rule holds for a type parameter too: `T.m(…)` reaches only what a trait supplies
// **without a receiver**. `Thing` implements `label` with a body that reads `self`, which makes it
// an instance method — so a `T` has nothing to bind `self` to, and there is no value at the call to
// become one.
//
// Reported at the DEFINITION site, against the implementor that makes it impossible, rather than at
// whichever call site happens to instantiate `T = Thing`: whether a method needs `self` is derived
// from the implementation's body, per implementor, so a generic body writing `T.label()` is
// promising something every implementor must keep. The body is what is wrong.
// expect: error E0047 at 17:42
// expect: exit 1
trait Named { fn label(): string }
struct Thing {
    v: int
    impl Named { fn label(): string { return "thing ${self.v}" } }
}
fn describe<T: Named>(): string { return T.label() }
echo describe::<Thing>()
